•	Docker: Used to containerize the three-tier application and create Docker images for deployment. 
•	Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR): Used as a private container registry to securely store and manage Docker images. 
•	ArgoCD: Deployed on Amazon EKS to enable GitOps-based continuous deployment. 
 Integrated with GitHub to automatically synchronize application manifests with the cluster state. 
•	Kubernetes (Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service – EKS): Used for container orchestration, scaling, and high availability of the application. 
 kubectl is configured on the Jenkins server to interact with the EKS cluster. 
•	SonarQube: Integrated into the CI/CD pipeline for static code analysis and code quality enforcement. 
•	Trivy: Used for container image vulnerability scanning to identify security issues before deployment. 
•	AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM): Used to manage secure access control for AWS resources and services. 
•	AWS Web Application Firewall (WAF): Used to protect the application from common web-based attacks. 
•	Amazon CloudWatch: Used for centralized logging, monitoring, and performance analysis of the application and infrastructure. 
•	Amazon CloudTrail:  CloudTrail is used to track AWS API activity and infrastructure-related changes. 
 
 	 	 	 	 	 	 	 	 	 
B) Security and Code Quality Tools: 
 
●	SonarQube 
Installed and configured on the CI/CD server to perform static code analysis and ensure code quality, maintainability, and early detection of vulnerabilities. 
 
●	Trivy 
Integrated into the Jenkins pipeline to perform container image vulnerability scanning, identifying known security issues before deployment. 
 
●	OWASP ZAP 
           Used for dynamic application security testing (DAST) after deployment.      
 
    
C)  Networking and Access Control 
 
•	CI/CD Server to Amazon EKS Communication 
Ensure that the Jenkins server can securely communicate with the Amazon EKS cluster. This requires proper network configuration, security group rules, and IAM permissions to allow interaction with the Kubernetes API. 
 
•	GitHub Access 
The GitHub repository must be accessible to both Jenkins and ArgoCD. Webhooks are configured to trigger Jenkins builds on code changes, and a GitHub Personal Access Token (PAT) is used for secure authentication. 
 
•	Container Registry Access 
Jenkins must have permission to authenticate with Amazon Elastic 
Container Registry (ECR) to push and pull Docker images. Appropriate IAM roles and credentials are configured to ensure secure image management. 
 
D) Basic Configuration Knowledge 
•	Git and GitHub 
Basic knowledge of Git and GitHub for source code management, including repository handling and webhook configuration to trigger Jenkins pipelines. 
 
•	Jenkins Pipeline 
Understanding of Jenkins pipelines and writing Jenkinsfile scripts to automate build, security checks, and deployment workflows. 
 
 	 	 	 	 	 	 	 	 	 
 
•	Docker 
Ability to write Dockerfiles to containerize applications and manage container images. Basic knowledge of Docker commands for image build and push operations. 
 
•	Kubernetes (Amazon EKS) 
Fundamental understanding of Kubernetes concepts and deployment using kubectl, including working with namespaces, pods, deployments, services, and ingress resources through YAML configuration files. 
 
•	ArgoCD 
Basic understanding of GitOps principles and how ArgoCD manages application deployment by syncing Kubernetes manifests from a Git repository to the Amazon EKS cluster. 
 
•	Cloud and Security Basics 
Familiarity with AWS IAM roles, security groups, and basic cloud networking concepts is required to securely access and manage Amazon EKS resources. 
 
E) Access and Credentials 
●	GitHub Credentials 
Access to a GitHub repository for storing application source code and Kubernetes manifests. 
A	GitHub Personal Access Token (PAT) or SSH key is configured in Jenkins for secure repository access. 
 
●	Container Registry Credentials 
Access to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) for pushing and pulling Docker images. 
Jenkins is configured with appropriate AWS IAM roles or credentials to authenticate with ECR. 
 
●	Kubernetes and ArgoCD Credentials 
A	valid kubeconfig file is required to interact with the Amazon EKS cluster using kubectl. 
 Jenkins must have the necessary permissions to trigger deployments through ArgoCD, either via API tokens or Git-based synchronization. 
 
 
                        
●	SonarQube 
Configure secure authentication for SonarQube and enforce quality gates within the Jenkins pipeline to ensure code quality, maintainability, and early detection of security issues. 
 
●	Container Security (Trivy) 
Integrate Trivy into the CI/CD pipeline to perform container image vulnerability scanning before deployment, ensuring that images with critical vulnerabilities are not promoted to production. 
 
●	Access Control and Permissions 
Implement AWS IAM roles and policies to enforce least-privilege access for Jenkins, Amazon EKS, ArgoCD, and Amazon ECR. 
 
●	Application and Infrastructure Protection 
Use AWS Web Application Firewall (WAF) to protect the application from common web attacks and malicious traffic. 
 
●	Monitoring and Alerts 
 Utilize Amazon CloudWatch to monitor application logs, system metrics, and cluster health. Configure alarms to detect abnormal behavior and performance issues. 
 
 
 
     3. OVERALL DESCRIPTION 
     3.1 INTRODUCTION: 
   In today’s fast-paced software development environment, the need for rapid, reliable, and secure application deployment has become critical. Traditional deployment approaches that rely heavily on manual processes often lead to configuration errors, delayed releases, and increased security risks. To address these challenges, modern organizations adopt Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) practices that automate the software delivery lifecycle. 
 
  This project, titled “End-to-End CI/CD Pipeline for Secure Three-Tier Application Deployment on Amazon EKS,” focuses on designing and implementing a fully automated DevSecOps CI/CD pipeline for deploying a cloud-native three-tier application. The solution utilizes Jenkins for pipeline automation, Docker for application containerization, and Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) for scalable and reliable container orchestration. ArgoCD is used to enable GitOps-based deployment, ensuring declarative, version-controlled, and synchronized application delivery. 
 
   To enhance code quality and security, the pipeline integrates SonarQube for static code analysis and Trivy for container image vulnerability scanning. Secure access control and application protection are enforced using AWS IAM and AWS Web Application Firewall (WAF). Additionally, Amazon CloudWatch is employed for centralized logging and monitoring, providing visibility into application performance and system health. 
 
   Security and code quality are treated as integral components of the pipeline. SonarQube is used for static code analysis to detect code smells, bugs, and maintainability issues, while Trivy performs container image vulnerability scanning to identify known security risks before deployment. Access control and infrastructure security are enforced using AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM), and AWS Web Application Firewall (WAF) is implemented to protect the application from common web-based attacks. For operational visibility, Amazon CloudWatch is utilized to collect logs and metrics, enabling continuous monitoring of application performance and system health. 
 
 
 
 
 	 
 
 
 
3.2 DATA FLOW DIAGRAM 
 
            Stage 1 :-   From Github to Jenkins CI Pipeline 
 
  
 	Stage 2:- Continuous Deployment on Amazon EKS 
  
              
 
 
 
 
Stage 3:- Logging ,Monitoring and DAST Testing 
  
 
The code changes automatically trigger a Jenkins pipeline that builds, tests, and analyzes the project. Upon successful completion of the build process, a Docker image is created and subsequently deployed to a Kubernetes cluster using Argo CD for continuous delivery. In the event of build failures, detailed reports are generated to provide insights into the issues encountered, facilitating quicker resolution and improving overall development efficiency. 
 
        3.3 ARCHITECTURE 
 
This project implements a secure, automated DevSecOps CI/CD architecture on AWS, integrating continuous integration, continuous delivery, and continuous security across the application lifecycle 
1.	Source Code Management & Triggering 
The application source code is maintained in GitHub. 
Every code commit or pull request triggers the CI pipeline automatically using GitHub Webhooks. 
This ensures no manual intervention and enforces consistency. 
Why this matters: 
 If CI is not auto-triggered, your pipeline is already broken. 
 
2.	Continuous Integration (CI) – Jenkins on EKS 
 
Jenkins is deployed as a pod inside Amazon EKS, backed by Amazon EBS via PVC. 
The CI pipeline performs the following stages in strict order: 
 
a)	Unit Testing 
 
●	Validates application logic early 
 
●	Fails fast if code quality is poor 
 
b)	Secret Scanning – GitLeaks 
●	Scans source code for exposed credentials (API keys, passwords, tokens) 
●	Pipeline fails immediately if secrets are detected 
 
c)	Dependency Vulnerability Scan – OWASP Dependency Check 
●	Scans third-party libraries for known CVEs 
 
●	Prevents vulnerable dependencies from reaching production 
 
d)	Docker Image Build 
●	Builds the application into a Docker image 
 
●	Uses minimal base images to reduce attack surface 
 
e)	Container Image Scan – Trivy 
●	Scans the built image for OS-level and application-level vulnerabilities 
 
●	Enforces severity thresholds before allowing image push 
 
 
f)	Image Push to Amazon ECR 
●	Only security-approved images are pushed 
 
●	Amazon ECR acts as the trusted container registry. 
 
 
3.	GitOps-Based Continuous Deployment (CD) 
Deployment is handled using GitOps, not Jenkins, pushing manifests directly. 
●	Kubernetes manifests are stored in a separate GitOps repository. 
 
●	Image tag updates are committed automatically after a successful build. 
 
●	Argo CD continuously monitors the GitOps repo and syncs changes to EKS. 
 
Why GitOps: 
●	Version-controlled deployments 
 
●	Full audit trail 
 
●	Easy rollback 
 
●	Zero manual kubectl access 
 
4.	Kubernetes Deployment on Amazon EKS 
The application runs on Amazon EKS, split into: 
●	Frontend Pods 
 
●	Backend Pods 
 
Traffic is managed using: 
●	Ingress Controller (ALB Ingress) 
 
●	AWS WAF is integrated at the load balancer level to block common attacks (SQLi,  
XSS) 
 
 
5.	Database Layer 
The backend connects to Amazon RDS, configured with: 
●	Encryption at rest 
 
●	Private subnet deployment 
 
●	No public access 
 
This isolates the database from the internet entirely. 
 
 
6.	Continuous Security Testing (DAST) 
After deployment, OWASP ZAP performs Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST)  on the live application. 
●	Detects runtime vulnerabilities 
 
●	Simulates real-world attacker behavior 
 
7.	Monitoring & Observability 
a)	Amazon CloudWatch 
●	Application logs 
 
●	Pod and node metrics 
 
●	CPU, memory, and error-based alarms 
 
b)	Amazon CloudTrail 
●	Tracks IAM changes 
 
●	Logs AWS API calls 
